## Running the demo

```sh
make demo   # python -m app.main --offline — every tool call dispatches to its mock
make eval   # run the copilot against the bundled fixture task list
make check  # compile-check + test the generated project
```

`make demo` never touches the network: every tool's mock stands in for
the real payer/EHR/scheduling integration, exactly as `ack demo --offline`
does for every agenticcarekit blueprint.

## Scope — read this before you extend it

This blueprint produces **decision support** for administrative work:
documentation drafting, navigation between payers/specialists, and
scheduling logistics. It does **not** diagnose and does **not** recommend
or select treatment.

**Prior authorization is drafting only.** `draft_prior_auth` produces
paperwork for a human to review and submit through your organization's
real channel — it never auto-submits anything, to any payer, under any
configuration. If you are tempted to wire this straight through to a
payer's submission API, that is a deliberate design boundary this
template does not cross; add a human-in-the-loop step instead.

All bundled sample data (payers, patients, specialists, slots) is
synthetic. Do not point this demo at real PHI without first configuring
a real redactor and a policy that matches your organization's
requirements.

## Ejectable: this is your code now

Nothing in this project imports "the framework" and calls back into it.
`app/` is plain Python that happens to import `agenticcarekit.kernel` and
`agenticcarekit.capabilities` the same way any other dependency is
imported. Delete `agenticcarekit` from `pyproject.toml`, inline the bits
you use, and it still runs. Generated once, owned forever.
